## 2. What scale-out cold start actually is

Our 48s is the tail of a longer chain. Estimates, to be measured once in each region:

| Step | Typical | Lever |
|---|---|---|
| EC2 launch of a g6e instance to SSH-ready | 60–120 s | ASG **warm pool** (stopped instances resume in ~30–60 s) |
| `docker pull` of a ~7 GB image from same-region ECR | 40–120 s | **AMI with the image pre-pulled**; smaller image (prep/runtime split) |
| Entrypoint: models + avatar cache from S3 | 28 s → ~5–13 s after §4 Q5/Q6/M1 | shrink payload under the 3.5 GB burst |
| Python start to listening | 15–20 s | Q3, Q4, later ONNX |
| **Total today** | **~3–5 min** | |
| **With warm pool + baked AMI + shrunk payload** | **~60–90 s** | |
| **With N+1 spare per region** | **0 s user-visible**, ~$1.9/hr per region | the only way to hide it entirely |

Two things follow. First, an instance-level plan (AMI bake, ASG warm pool, N+1
policy, rolling deploy) buys more than any further Python work, and it is infra
config, not code. Second, image updates should never cold-start a serving GPU:
rolling deploy with one spare does that today without any code change.

**Decision needed:** target seconds from scale signal to serving, per region, and
whether one idle spare per region is acceptable cost. Everything in §4 M4 hangs on it.

### The relay / GPU split (owner's untested idea)

What exists now: `relay/main.py` is `main_server.py` in blank-avatar mode, the
*same process*. The `relay/` and `avatar/` packages are module boundaries, not
services. There is no relay that talks to a GPU process today.

Two ways to split, with very different latency risk:

| Design | Who owns WebRTC + audio playback | What crosses the boundary | Added latency | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | GPU process | relay → GPU: model PCM in 120 ms chunks + control events, over a Unix socket / gRPC on the same host | < 1 ms local, ~1 ms same-AZ | low: A/V sync stays in one process, as now |
| **B** | relay process | GPU → relay: rendered 720×720 frames at 25 fps (~39 MB/s) | fine over shared memory, poor over a network | high: cross-process A/V sync, a new frame transport |

The developer's "latency will be high" concern is right for B over a network and
wrong for A on one host. The feature step is already 120 ms; a 1 ms hop is noise.

What A buys: the GPU image changes rarely (models, render code), so most deploys
touch only the relay and never cold-start a GPU. What it does **not** buy: faster
scale-out (the GPU image still has to boot on a new instance). Cost: a protocol
between the two, two processes to supervise, and the greeting path crosses a
socket (measure it; expect +1–2 ms).

**Owner's reading, confirmed 2026-09-16:** same machine ≈ in-process; only a
network hop between hosts would cost anything. So this is worth an experiment.

**Experiment E1 (cheap first step, before any design work).** The CPU-only relay
already runs `main_server` with video disabled by flag (locally today), and the
GPU build enables video by flag. That is the harness: run two processes on one
GPU box — the relay process in blank mode, a GPU process with video — and stream
120 ms PCM chunks from the first to the second over a Unix socket. Measure (a)
socket round-trip histogram, (b) greeting time, (c) time to first rendered
frame, against the single-process numbers (774 ms greeting). Expected: +1–2 ms.
If that holds, design A proceeds; if it does not, the reason is in the
histogram, not in a debate.

**Recommendation:** run E1 any time; do the real split as design A *after*
backlog 04 (module extraction) has moved the code out of `main_server.py`, at
which point the split is a transport decision, not a refactor. Gate: greeting
≤ 800 ms and `quality_gate` IDENTICAL before/after.

